  "query_preview": "..\n\nRest of Today through Friday...\n\nWeak cold front moved through the area overnight and has swept\ncloud cover out of the area, leaving sunny skies and diminishing\nnortherly winds this afternoon. Drier air, clear skies and \ngenerally light winds will allow temperatures to drop into the 35 \nto 45 degree range across much of the region tonight, coolest over\nthe western valleys. Probabilities for falling under 35 degrees in\nthe western valleys is under 10%, but a frost and local freeze are\nnot out of the question. If you have tender vegetation you may\nwant to protect it tonight. \n\nSunday looks sunny and warm with temperatures rebounding from the\ncool start to reach the mid to upper 70s by afternoon. \n\nMonday is looking very warm and breezy. By very warm temperatures\nare pushing 30 year return intervals for this time of year, and\nrecord high temperatures in the mid 80s to around 90 degrees are\nin the forecast for Livingston, Billings, and Sheridan. NBM\nprobability for highs above 90 degrees are in the 15 to 30 percent\nrange in central areas, with areas southeast of Billings into\nnortheast Wyoming showing pockets of 30 to 70% probabilities, \nhighest in the major river valleys. Downslope westerly winds \ngusting 20 to 30 mph are the culprit for the very warm \ntemperatures, and will also contribute to elevated fire weather \nconditions across the area. Be extra cautious with any activities\nthat could spark a grass fire Monday. \n\nA mainly dry cold front late Monday night into Tuesday will drop\ntemperatures by around 10 degrees for Tuesday, with abundant\nsunshine and less wind compared to Monday. Wednesday and Thursday\nare near carbon copies of Tuesday, with highs in the 70s under \nmostly sunny skies. \n\nPattern changes may develop heading into the weekend. Currently\nabout 14% of ensembles are clustered around a backdoor cold front\nwith cooler wetter conditions dropping into the area. By Saturday\n62% of ensemble members are indicating a trof in the area, and 77%\nby Sunday. Given the time of year (Fall is challenging for the\nmodels) and the abundance of tropical systems in the Pacific\n(impact upstream patterns) there will likely be a lot of changes\nto the forecast for next weekend, but at least there is a\npotential break in the very warm dry and breezy pattern that will\nbe over the area through this week. Please be very careful to not\nstart a grass fire this week as the conditions will support easy\nignitions and quick spread of any fires that do get going,\nespecially on Monday. Chambers",
